# Calibration

{% if calibration.calibration %}Calibration: **{{ calibration.calibration }}**{% if calibration.generator %}, its answers generated by an {{ calibration.generator }}{% endif %}.{% if calibration.shares %} Rows by slice: {% for k, v in calibration.shares.items() %}{{ k }} {{ '{0:0.2f}'.format(v) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}{% if calibration.doc_kinds %}; English document kinds {{ calibration.doc_kinds }}{% endif %}{% if calibration.cal_rows %}; {{ calibration.cal_rows }} rows of {{ calibration.cal_cols }} tokens{% endif %}.{% endif %}

The calibration covers what models read and write at work rather than only web text: documents pasted into user turns and returned as tool results (prose, code, and real commits as diffs in 30 languages and formats), short tool-use loops, a little of the model's own voice, and the default corpus. Its answers come from an uncalibrated 8-bit quant of the same model, so no calibration is baked into the calibration data. Against the default calibration at equal size, the mix cut excess KLD on real user prompts by 12-13% on Qwen3.8-27B and 8-11% on Ornith-1.5-9B (4 bpw), at no cost in bytes. How each part was chosen and measured is in [docs/calibration.md](https://github.com/yeasah/vllm-exl3-plugin/blob/main/docs/calibration.md).

# Memory

The card's VRAM columns are lower than the checkpoint size because of how the input embeddings{% if multimodal %} and the vision encoder{% endif %} are handled. The embeddings are not quantized, because EXL3 is not an efficient format for them; the two platforms deal with that differently:
- exllamav3 typically offloads the input embeddings to CPU, which costs some speed but removes them from VRAM entirely.
- vllm-exl3-plugin typically quantizes them at load time to `blockq`, a cheap, embedding-compatible format: resident in VRAM, but much smaller.
{% if multimodal %}

The vision encoder is left unquantized, preserving full vision fidelity against the base model, which is otherwise harder to characterize. It can add significantly to VRAM; two ways to avoid that:
- CPU offload for the encoder, which costs little for text-heavy use.
- Omitting the encoder entirely, for text-only use.
{% endif %}
